Actions not considered risk factors for the transmission of bloodborne pathogens include casual contact like shaking hands, touching contaminated surfaces, and consuming water or food. Droplet transmission through coughing or sneezing is typically associated with respiratory pathogens, not bloodborne ones. Lastly, bloodborne pathogens are not transmitted through indirect contact with contaminated objects, as they require direct access to the entry sites mentioned (broken skin, mucous membranes, or respiratory system).
