 Hypovolaemic Shock.  
 Cardiogenic Shock.  
 Anaphylactic Shock.  
  
Hypovolaemic Shock  
Hypo  = low   vol = volume   aemic  = blood  
  
Hypovolaemic shock is caused by loss of bodily fluids, the result of which is 
low blood volume.  Hypovolaemic shock is usually caused by:  
 External bleeding.  
 Internal bleeding.  
 Burns.  
 V omiting and diarrhoea.  
 Excessive sweating.  
  
Signs and symptoms  
Usually the first response is a release of adrenaline which will cause:  
 Pulse  rate to rise.  
 Pale, clammy skin.  For dark skinned casualties check the colour of the skin 
inside the lips.
--- End chunk 4 ---
